 "literal": "Fish having sold, paddy having heaped, / from the boats on top, the houses are stirred up / by the pepper bags surrounding the houses, / the shore is bustling with loud noise / the gold presents brought by ships, / reaching the shore by backwater boats, / mountain products and sea products, / gathering together, giving to those who come, / Kuttuvan of the golden garland, with water-like toddy, / like Muciri with its roaring sea like a drum, / even if excellent wealth full of goodness is given with humility, / 'she will not marry those who are not worthy' saying, / the father also does not give; if so, those who came, / the siege ladders pitched to succeed, in that place, / will it suffer indeed, sighing like a vulture / on the inner walls where it rests, / the great good town where armies clash in a difficult path?",
 "translation": "Selling fish and heaping up paddy, the houses are stirred up by boats on top. The shore is in a bustle with the noise of pepper bags surrounding the houses. The gold brought by ships is brought to the shore by backwater boats. Mountain and sea products are gathered together and given to those who come by Kuttuvan of the golden garland, who possesses water-like toddy. Like his port city Muciri, where the roaring sea sounds like a drum, is this girl. Even if suitors offer immense wealth with great humility, her father refuses to give her, saying she will not marry those who are not worthy. If so, in that place where the suitors have pitched their siege ladders to succeed, will this great, good town suffer, where armies clash in difficult paths and vultures rest on the inner walls, sighing?",
